## Solution

Let $E_k$ denote the expected number of additional days needed when $k$ reactors are still dormant. We want $E_6$, with $E_0 = 0$.

**Recurrence.** When $k$ reactors are dormant, on the next day each independently activates with probability $1/2$. The number that activate is $\text{Binomial}(k, 1/2)$. If $j$ activate, we used one day and have $k-j$ remaining dormant. Including the $j=0$ case (no progress) on the right side:

$$E_k = 1 + \sum_{j=0}^{k}\binom{k}{j}2^{-k}E_{k-j}.$$

The $j=0$ term contributes $2^{-k}E_k$, so

$$E_k = \frac{1 + \sum_{j=1}^{k}\binom{k}{j}2^{-k}E_{k-j}}{1 - 2^{-k}}.$$

**Computing step by step.**

- $E_1 = \dfrac{1 + \binom{1}{1}\cdot\frac12\cdot E_0}{1-\frac12} = \dfrac{1}{1/2} = 2.$

- $E_2 = \dfrac{1 + 2\cdot\frac14\cdot E_1}{1-\frac14} = \dfrac{1+1}{3/4} = \dfrac{8}{3}.$

- $E_3 = \dfrac{1 + 3\cdot\frac18\cdot\frac83 + 3\cdot\frac18\cdot 2}{1-\frac18} = \dfrac{1+1+\frac34}{7/8} = \dfrac{11/4}{7/8} = \dfrac{22}{7}.$

- $E_4 = \dfrac{1 + 4\cdot\frac1{16}\cdot\frac{22}{7} + 6\cdot\frac1{16}\cdot\frac83 + 4\cdot\frac1{16}\cdot 2}{1-\frac1{16}} = \dfrac{1+\frac{11}{14}+1+\frac12}{15/16} = \dfrac{23/7}{15/16} = \dfrac{368}{105}.$

- $E_5$: the inner sum is
  $$5\cdot\tfrac1{32}\cdot\tfrac{368}{105}+10\cdot\tfrac1{32}\cdot\tfrac{22}{7}+10\cdot\tfrac1{32}\cdot\tfrac83+5\cdot\tfrac1{32}\cdot 2 = \tfrac{23}{42}+\tfrac{55}{56}+\tfrac56+\tfrac{5}{16}=\tfrac{899}{336}.$$
  So $E_5 = \dfrac{1+899/336}{31/32} = \dfrac{1235/336}{31/32} = \dfrac{2470}{651}.$

- $E_6$: the inner sum is
  $$6\cdot\tfrac1{64}\cdot\tfrac{2470}{651}+15\cdot\tfrac1{64}\cdot\tfrac{368}{105}+20\cdot\tfrac1{64}\cdot\tfrac{22}{7}+15\cdot\tfrac1{64}\cdot\tfrac83+6\cdot\tfrac1{64}\cdot 2.$$
  Computing each term:
  $$\tfrac{1235}{3472}+\tfrac{23}{28}+\tfrac{55}{56}+\tfrac58+\tfrac{3}{16}.$$
  Using common denominator $3472 = 2^4\cdot 7\cdot 31$:
  $$\tfrac{1235+2852+3410+2170+651}{3472}=\tfrac{10318}{3472}=\tfrac{737}{248}.$$
  Therefore
  $$E_6 = \frac{1+737/248}{1-1/64}=\frac{985/248}{63/64}=\frac{985\cdot 64}{248\cdot 63}=\frac{985\cdot 8}{31\cdot 63}=\frac{7880}{1953}.$$

**Coprimality check.** $7880 = 2^3\cdot 5\cdot 197$ and $1953 = 3^2\cdot 7\cdot 31$. These share no common prime factor, so $\gcd(m,n)=1$.

Thus $m = 7880$, $n = 1953$, and

$$100m + n = 788000 + 1953 = \boxed{789953}.$$

